Z||000005||Monkeyman||11-01-2001||07:35 PM||||But is the front suspension the same?  Do all '88s have the vented rotors?||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||11-01-2001||07:57 PM||||For the dolly.... you really don't need or want any uprights on it, the cradle will just end up sliding off of it. All you need is a flat square frame for the cradle to sit on. It can be considerably smaller than the cradle, as long as one dimension is long enough to reach each side or front and back of the cradle. The two that I use are about 12" high, just enough to be able to get under the car to take out the last bolts, but no so high that you cannot lift the car over it. I'll take some pictures tomorow and give some recommendations on dolly size.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000007||Patrick's Dad||11-01-2001||09:29 PM||||Monk: No problem about the thread. All 88's have vented rotors and essentially the same suspension, except as noted. The fronts are the same, as are the rest of the brakes. It wouldn't have made $ense to do it otherwise for 26K cars.<P>Rock: Can't wait for the pics. How would the cradle slip off four uprights that held it level?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick's Dad||11-02-2001||05:32 PM||||Bump.||||reg||1
Z||000009||rockcrawl||11-03-2001||10:21 PM||||OK, here are the pics I promised. I have a few that I use, but this is my favorite, I've used it at least a dozen times. It has two fixed wheels and two wheels that swivel and are attached to a handle for steering. I didn't make it, it's probably been around longer than I have.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dolly1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dolly2.jpg"> <BR>and shown in action tonight!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh2.jpg"> <BR>The front crossmember of the cradle sits on the board across the fixed wheels. The rear crossmember sits on the moveable board. In this case it is sitting on the trans pan because it's been highly modified, but you get the idea. The handle makes it really easy to manuever in and out of the car. <BR>The narrow width of the dolly gives plenty of room to take the cradle bolts out. I think I misunderstood what you were saying about the uprights. By corners I thought you meant where the cradle mounting bolts are. That wouldn't leave you any room to get the bolts out. Four uprights at the corners where the crossmembers meet the side rails would be fine. That would also leave you room to get the trans mounts of if need be.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick's Dad||11-04-2001||08:24 PM||||rock: Your engine is longitudinal...! That also doesn't look like a stock cradle.<P>The intent of my idea was to use the 4x4 uprights at the corners of the cradle, where they meet the rest of the car. I can see where this would interfere with bolt removal. I like your cradle. Is the movable board simply loose, and gets pinned by the weight of the engine/trans/cradle? This laso looks a bit less expensive than the cradle I had thunk up.||||reg||5
Z||000011||rockcrawl||11-04-2001||09:20 PM||||That cradle has been hatchetized, tomorow I'll show you how a stock '88 cradle sits on there and it'll make more sense. I didn't make the cart, but I couldn't have designed it much better for this application, it works great. I'll get some measurements for ya too. ||||reg||1
